// prism_ui_form -- the first prism/ui/ composition module (PRISM_ROADMAP.md milestone MB.1,
// PRISM_STATUS.md: "typed fields + validation + error display -- where Ant's value actually
// lives"). A form is a COMPOSITION of the closed 22-primitive vocabulary (entry/pick/flag/range
// from widget/prism_interact.nova, stack/label from widget/prism_arrange.nova + prism_content.nova)
// -- this file invents no PrismNodeKind and emits no markup itself (axiom A7; §15.1). Everything
// here is a NOVA value or a plain function over prism_node.nova's Result<PrismNode>, exactly the
// layering widget/README.md establishes for MA.3 and that ui/README.md says MB.1 builds on now
// that widget/ + style/ are frozen.
//
// ── DESIGN DECISION: field kind is a closed enum carrying its OWN value+config, not a dict ──────
// Seven form-field kinds: text, email, password, number, range, choice, toggle. Each maps to
// exactly one widget/ primitive (entry x4 kinds, range, pick, flag) -- so `PrismFormFieldKind`
// is a 7-variant enum where EACH VARIANT carries the payload that kind actually needs (a plain
// string for the three entry-text kinds, `value/min/max` for number, `value/min/max/step` for
// range, `options/selected` for choice, `state` for toggle). This is the "one typed constructor
// per kind, no stringly-typed kind parameter" rule MA.3 established (prism_interact.nova's
// PrismEntryKind, PrismLinkTarget) applied one layer up: an invalid field kind, or a number field
// missing its bounds, is unrepresentable rather than a runtime surprise.
//
// ── DESIGN DECISION: a field's constructor validates its SHAPE, not always its CURRENT VALUE ────
// This is the one genuinely subtle call in this file, made after tracing what each underlying
// widget can and cannot RENDER:
//   - `prism_form_number`'s min/max is validated (min<=max is a property of the field's
//     DEFINITION, independent of any particular value) but an out-of-range `value` is NOT
//     rejected at construction. Why: the underlying widget is `entry` (HTML <input type=number>),
//     which can display ANY int the user actually typed, in range or not -- that is the whole
//     point of a form: it must be constructible WITH the user's current, possibly-wrong input, so
//     `prism_form_render` can show that exact value next to an error message. Rejecting it at
//     construction would make "show the user what they typed, plus why it's wrong" impossible.
//   - `prism_form_range` and `prism_form_choice`, by contrast, delegate FULLY to
//     `prism_interact.prism_range` / `prism_pick` at construction (value/min/max/step, or
//     selected-must-be-a-real-option, all validated immediately). Why the asymmetry: a slider
//     thumb must physically sit somewhere on the track, and a <select> can only ever show one of
//     its own <option>s -- neither widget has a way to RENDER an "invalid" state the way a text
//     box can just print whatever string it was given. There is no user-facing benefit to
//     deferring their validation, and prism_interact's own constructors already reject bad shapes
//     -- reusing that (CLAUDE.md: "does this already exist?") is strictly better than
//     re-deriving the same min<=max/step>0/value-in-range logic here a second time.
//   - Consequence for validation rules (below): Number and Range therefore accept NO rule from
//     `PrismFormRule` (their one meaningful constraint -- the numeric bound -- is already part of
//     their own kind payload, checked at construction for Range and at `prism_form_validate` time
//     for Number). Choice accepts `required` (an empty `selected` is a legitimate "nothing chosen
//     yet" state, per prism_pick's own doc) but not length rules (a select's value is a fixed
//     internal code the caller controls via `options`, not free text -- "min length 5 on a combo
//     box" is not a meaningful constraint and is rejected as a caller mistake rather than a
//     silent no-op). Toggle accepts `required`, meaning "must be true" (the very common
//     "must accept terms" pattern) -- but not length rules, which have no meaning on a bool.
//
// ── DESIGN DECISION: validation rules are a 3-variant closed enum, explicitly pattern-free ──────
// `required` / `min_length(n)` / `max_length(n)`. No regex, no format validation (an "email
// format" rule would need a pattern engine this milestone deliberately does not add -- `email`
// only changes the entry KIND passed to `prism_entry`, i.e. the keyboard/input-mode hint, not
// what gets validated). Rules are attached to an already-built field via `prism_form_field_with_rules`
// (REPLACES the field's rule set; it is not additive -- call it once with the full set you want,
// matching the immutable "returns a new value" idiom used throughout prism/widget/) rather than
// being a constructor parameter, because every field constructor's signature is fixed by this
// milestone's spec (`prism_form_text(name, label, value)` etc. -- 3/3/3/5/6/4/4 args, full arity,
// no defaults per NOVA_LANGUAGE_FEATURES.md trap 12) and rules are optional, composable, and
// orthogonal to the value/shape the constructor itself validates.
//
// ── DESIGN DECISION: `rules` is `list<PrismFormRule>`, a TYPED list, not bare `list` ─────────────
// This deliberately departs from prism_interact.nova's own convention (`prism_pick`'s
// `options: list` + a runtime `type_name(o) != "PrismPickOption"` guard) for a documented reason:
// `PrismFormRule` is an ENUM, and NOVA_LANGUAGE_FEATURES.md trap 10 records that an enum VARIANT's
// static type is the variant, not the enum -- which casts real doubt on whether `type_name()` at
// RUNTIME would ever report the enum's own name for an enum value the way it reliably does for a
// STRUCT (verified working: PrismPickOption, PrismTabPanel, PrismNode, PrismFormField below).
// A runtime check that might silently never fire is worse than no check. `list<PrismFormRule>` is
// declared and consumed in THIS SAME FILE, mirroring the one place a same-file, non-self-
// referential, few-variant enum generic IS verified to work end to end: prism_arrange.nova's
// `prism_mesh(cols: list<PrismMeshTrack>, ...)`. `PrismFormRule` matches that shape exactly
// (same-file, not self-referential, 3 tiny variants), so the type system enforces homogeneity at
// compile time instead of a maybe-broken runtime guard -- a caller literally cannot pass the wrong
// shape. `PrismFormFieldKind.FormFieldChoice`'s `options` stays bare `list` on purpose: its
// elements are `PrismPickOption`, a STRUCT declared in a DIFFERENT file (prism_interact.nova), and
// this file already reuses `prism_interact.prism_pick`'s own (struct-based, verified-working)
// runtime check by calling it at construction -- see above -- so no second check is needed here.
//
// ── DESIGN DECISION: help text and error text both render through `prism_label`, never markup ──
// A field also carries optional help text (`prism_form_field_with_help`, "" = none, matching
// prism_link_href/prism_pick_selected_value's own established "" -as-absence convention rather
// than introducing `string?` for a case this codebase already has a consistent idiom for). Both
// help and any validation error message are plain, caller/derived TEXT with no special status --
// composed into the row via `prism_content.prism_label`, exactly like every other piece of text
// in this file. There is no error-rendering code path that touches markup; escaping (if any target
// needs it) is entirely `prism_render_html.nova`'s job at its one sink, per §15.1/A3 -- this file
// never inspects, escapes, or otherwise treats error/help text differently from any other label.
//
// ── DESIGN DECISION: duplicate field names are rejected at `prism_form(fields)`, not per-field ──
// A single field has no visibility into its siblings, so the check belongs at the point where the
// full field list is known -- mirrors `prism_pick`'s duplicate-option-value and `prism_grid`'s
// duplicate-column-name checks (same file-local "seen dict, reject the second occurrence, name it"
// idiom throughout widget/).
//
// ── CROSS-MODULE CONSTRUCTION (trap 11) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `PrismFormFieldKind`, `PrismFormRule`, `PrismFormField`, `PrismForm`, `PrismFormError` are all
// declared AND constructed only in this file, so no other file ever needs a bare variant/struct
// constructor for them -- every external touch point is one of the ordinary `prism_form_*`
// functions below, which is the wrapper-per-constructor pattern trap 11 requires, just satisfied
// by construction rather than needing a separate `prism_field_kind_*()` shim layer.
//
// PUBLIC API
//   Rules (closed, 3 kinds, pattern-free -- no regex)
//     PrismFormRule                                    Required() | MinLength(n) | MaxLength(n)
//     prism_form_rule_required() / _min_length(n) / _max_length(n)   cross-module ctors
//   Field constructors (all `Result<PrismFormField>`; name/label validated non-empty on every one)
//     prism_form_text(name, label, value)
//     prism_form_email(name, label, value)
//     prism_form_password(name, label, value)
//     prism_form_number(name, label, value, min_val, max_val)      min_val<=max_val enforced;
//                                                                    `value` may be out of range
//                                                                    (a validate-time concern)
//     prism_form_range(name, label, value, min_val, max_val, step_val)  delegates fully to
//                                                                    prism_interact.prism_range
//     prism_form_choice(name, label, options, selected)             delegates to prism_interact.prism_pick
//     prism_form_toggle(name, label, state)
//   Field composition (immutable "returns a new field" style)
//     prism_form_field_with_rules(field, rules: list<PrismFormRule>) -> Result<PrismFormField>
//       rejects: an incompatible rule for the field's kind, a duplicate rule TYPE, a negative
//       min_length/max_length, or min_length > max_length
//     prism_form_field_with_help(field, help: string) -> PrismFormField    "" = no help text
//   Field accessors
//     prism_form_field_name/_label/_help(f) -> string      prism_form_field_rules(f) -> list<PrismFormRule>
//     prism_form_field_kind_str(f) -> string      exhaustive: text|email|password|number|range|choice|toggle
//     prism_form_field_text_value(f, default_val) -> string        (text/email/password/choice-selected)
//     prism_form_field_number_value/_min/_max(f, default_val) -> int
//     prism_form_field_range_value/_min/_max/_step(f, default_val) -> int
//     prism_form_field_bool_value(f, default_val) -> bool
//     prism_form_field_choice_options(f) -> list        prism_form_field_choice_selected(f, default_val) -> string
//   Form
//     prism_form(fields: list) -> Result<PrismForm>          rejects a non-PrismFormField element
//                                                             and a duplicate field name
//     prism_form_fields(form) -> list      prism_form_field_count(form) -> int
//   Validation
//     prism_form_validate(form) -> Result<list>       list<PrismFormError>, empty = valid
//     PrismFormError { field_name: string, message: string }
//     prism_form_error_field(e) -> string      prism_form_error_message(e) -> string
//   Rendering (composes ONLY from widget/prism_arrange + widget/prism_content + widget/prism_interact)
//     prism_form_render_input(field) -> Result<PrismNode>     just the input widget, no label/row
//     prism_form_render(form) -> Result<PrismNode>            stack of rows; each row is a stack of
//                                                              label + input + help(if any) + error(if any)

import prism_node
import prism_interact
import prism_content
import prism_arrange

// ── Field kind vocabulary (closed, 7 kinds -- see header) ────────────────────────────────────
enum PrismFormFieldKind
    FormFieldText(value: string)
    FormFieldEmail(value: string)
    FormFieldPassword(value: string)
    FormFieldNumber(value: int, min_val: int, max_val: int)
    FormFieldRange(value: int, min_val: int, max_val: int, step_val: int)
    FormFieldChoice(options: list, selected: string)
    FormFieldToggle(state: bool)

// ── Validation rule vocabulary (closed, 3 kinds, pattern-free -- see header) ─────────────────
enum PrismFormRule
    FormRuleRequired()
    FormRuleMinLength(n: int)
    FormRuleMaxLength(n: int)

fn prism_form_rule_required() -> PrismFormRule
    FormRuleRequired()
fn prism_form_rule_min_length(n: int) -> PrismFormRule
    FormRuleMinLength(n)
fn prism_form_rule_max_length(n: int) -> PrismFormRule
    FormRuleMaxLength(n)

// ── Values ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
type PrismFormField
    name: string
    label: string
    kind: PrismFormFieldKind
    rules: list<PrismFormRule>
    help: string

type PrismForm
    fields: list

type PrismFormError
    field_name: string
    message: string

// ── Field constructors ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Every one validates name/label non-empty first (an empty name can never round-trip a
// submission; an empty label would later fail prism_entry/prism_flag/prism_range's OWN
// non-empty-prompt/label requirement much further from the actual mistake -- rejecting here
// gives the caller a message that names the actual field constructor they called).

fn prism_form_text(name: string, label: string, value: string) -> Result<PrismFormField>
    if name == ""
        return err("prism_form_text: field name must not be empty")
    if label == ""
        return err("prism_form_text: needs a non-empty label (accessible name required)")
    ok(PrismFormField(name, label, FormFieldText(value), [], ""))

fn prism_form_email(name: string, label: string, value: string) -> Result<PrismFormField>
    if name == ""
        return err("prism_form_email: field name must not be empty")
    if label == ""
        return err("prism_form_email: needs a non-empty label (accessible name required)")
    ok(PrismFormField(name, label, FormFieldEmail(value), [], ""))

fn prism_form_password(name: string, label: string, value: string) -> Result<PrismFormField>
    if name == ""
        return err("prism_form_password: field name must not be empty")
    if label == ""
        return err("prism_form_password: needs a non-empty label (accessible name required)")
    ok(PrismFormField(name, label, FormFieldPassword(value), [], ""))

// min_val<=max_val is a property of the field's DEFINITION and is validated now; `value` being
// outside [min_val, max_val] is NOT rejected here -- see header design decision. That is what
// `prism_form_validate` catches.
fn prism_form_number(name: string, label: string, value: int, min_val: int, max_val: int) -> Result<PrismFormField>
    if name == ""
        return err("prism_form_number: field name must not be empty")
    if label == ""
        return err("prism_form_number: needs a non-empty label (accessible name required)")
    if min_val > max_val
        return err("prism_form_number: min (" + str(min_val) + ") must be <= max (" + str(max_val) + ")")
    ok(PrismFormField(name, label, FormFieldNumber(value, min_val, max_val), [], ""))

// Delegates entirely to prism_interact.prism_range (min<=max, step>0, value-in-range all
// enforced there) -- see header for why range's current value cannot be "constructed but
// invalid" the way a number entry's can.
fn prism_form_range(name: string, label: string, value: int, min_val: int, max_val: int, step_val: int) -> Result<PrismFormField>
    if name == ""
        return err("prism_form_range: field name must not be empty")
    if label == ""
        return err("prism_form_range: needs a non-empty label (accessible name required)")
    let check_r = prism_interact.prism_range(label, min_val, max_val, step_val, value)
    if is_err(check_r)
        return err("prism_form_range: " + unwrap_err(check_r))
    ok(PrismFormField(name, label, FormFieldRange(value, min_val, max_val, step_val), [], ""))

// Delegates entirely to prism_interact.prism_pick (option-list well-formedness, duplicate-value
// rejection, and selected-must-name-a-real-option all reused from there -- CLAUDE.md "does this
// already exist?"). `selected == ""` ("nothing chosen yet") is legitimate and passes through.
fn prism_form_choice(name: string, label: string, options: list, selected: string) -> Result<PrismFormField>
    if name == ""
        return err("prism_form_choice: field name must not be empty")
    if label == ""
        return err("prism_form_choice: needs a non-empty label (accessible name required)")
    let check_r = prism_interact.prism_pick(options, selected)
    if is_err(check_r)
        return err("prism_form_choice: " + unwrap_err(check_r))
    ok(PrismFormField(name, label, FormFieldChoice(options, selected), [], ""))

fn prism_form_toggle(name: string, label: string, state: bool) -> Result<PrismFormField>
    if name == ""
        return err("prism_form_toggle: field name must not be empty")
    if label == ""
        return err("prism_form_toggle: needs a non-empty label (accessible name required)")
    ok(PrismFormField(name, label, FormFieldToggle(state), [], ""))

// ── Rule <-> kind compatibility (private) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Exhaustive both ways (3 rules x 7 kinds = 21 arms, no wildcard): adding an 8th field kind or a
// 4th rule forces every arm here to be reconsidered, the same closed-vocabulary discipline
// PrismNodeKind/PrismMeshTrack/PrismIcon use elsewhere in prism/.

fn _prism_form_rule_compatible(kind: PrismFormFieldKind, rule: PrismFormRule) -> bool
    match rule
        FormRuleRequired() =>
            match kind
                FormFieldText(_) => true
                FormFieldEmail(_) => true
                FormFieldPassword(_) => true
                FormFieldChoice(_, _) => true
                FormFieldToggle(_) => true
                FormFieldNumber(_, _, _) => false
                FormFieldRange(_, _, _, _) => false
        FormRuleMinLength(_) =>
            match kind
                FormFieldText(_) => true
                FormFieldEmail(_) => true
                FormFieldPassword(_) => true
                FormFieldChoice(_, _) => false
                FormFieldToggle(_) => false
                FormFieldNumber(_, _, _) => false
                FormFieldRange(_, _, _, _) => false
        FormRuleMaxLength(_) =>
            match kind
                FormFieldText(_) => true
                FormFieldEmail(_) => true
                FormFieldPassword(_) => true
                FormFieldChoice(_, _) => false
                FormFieldToggle(_) => false
                FormFieldNumber(_, _, _) => false
                FormFieldRange(_, _, _, _) => false

fn _prism_form_rule_tag(r: PrismFormRule) -> string
    match r
        FormRuleRequired() => "required"
        FormRuleMinLength(_) => "min_length"
        FormRuleMaxLength(_) => "max_length"

fn _prism_form_rule_str(r: PrismFormRule) -> string
    match r
        FormRuleRequired() => "required"
        FormRuleMinLength(n) => "min_length(" + str(n) + ")"
        FormRuleMaxLength(n) => "max_length(" + str(n) + ")"

// ── Field composition ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// REPLACES `field.rules` wholesale (not additive) -- call once with the full rule set. Rejects,
// in order: a rule incompatible with this field's kind, a duplicate rule TYPE (ambiguous --
// "which min_length applies?"), a negative min_length/max_length, and min_length > max_length.
fn prism_form_field_with_rules(field: PrismFormField, rules: list<PrismFormRule>) -> Result<PrismFormField>
    let seen_tags = {}
    let mut min_len_seen = -1
    let mut max_len_seen = -1
    let mut idx = 0
    for r in rules
        let tag = _prism_form_rule_tag(r)
        if tag in seen_tags
            return err("prism_form_field_with_rules: duplicate rule type '" + tag + "' (rule " + str(idx) + ")")
        seen_tags[tag] = true
        if not _prism_form_rule_compatible(field.kind, r)
            return err("prism_form_field_with_rules: rule " + str(idx) + " (" + _prism_form_rule_str(r) + ") is not applicable to field '" + field.name + "' (kind " + prism_form_field_kind_str(field) + ")")
        match r
            FormRuleMinLength(n) =>
                if n < 0
                    return err("prism_form_field_with_rules: min_length must be >= 0, got " + str(n))
                min_len_seen = n
            FormRuleMaxLength(n) =>
                if n < 0
                    return err("prism_form_field_with_rules: max_length must be >= 0, got " + str(n))
                max_len_seen = n
            FormRuleRequired() => 0
        idx += 1
    if min_len_seen >= 0 and max_len_seen >= 0 and min_len_seen > max_len_seen
        return err("prism_form_field_with_rules: min_length (" + str(min_len_seen) + ") must be <= max_length (" + str(max_len_seen) + ")")
    ok(PrismFormField(field.name, field.label, field.kind, rules, field.help))

// Any string, including "", is a legitimate help text (same "any string is legitimate" stance
// prism_content.prism_label itself takes) -- infallible.
fn prism_form_field_with_help(field: PrismFormField, help: string) -> PrismFormField
    PrismFormField(field.name, field.label, field.kind, field.rules, help)

// ── Field accessors ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

fn prism_form_field_name(f: PrismFormField) -> string
    f.name

fn prism_form_field_label(f: PrismFormField) -> string
    f.label

fn prism_form_field_help(f: PrismFormField) -> string
    f.help

fn prism_form_field_rules(f: PrismFormField) -> list<PrismFormRule>
    f.rules

// Exhaustive, no wildcard -- see the closed-vocabulary discipline note above.
fn prism_form_field_kind_str(f: PrismFormField) -> string
    match f.kind
        FormFieldText(_) => "text"
        FormFieldEmail(_) => "email"
        FormFieldPassword(_) => "password"
        FormFieldNumber(_, _, _) => "number"
        FormFieldRange(_, _, _, _) => "range"
        FormFieldChoice(_, _) => "choice"
        FormFieldToggle(_) => "toggle"

// Value accessors mirror prism_node.nova's typed-getter-with-default convention: a caller-
// supplied default covers "this field is not that kind," never a crash or a magic sentinel.
fn prism_form_field_text_value(f: PrismFormField, default_val: string) -> string
    match f.kind
        FormFieldText(v) => v
        FormFieldEmail(v) => v
        FormFieldPassword(v) => v
        FormFieldChoice(_, sel) => sel
        FormFieldNumber(_, _, _) => default_val
        FormFieldRange(_, _, _, _) => default_val
        FormFieldToggle(_) => default_val

fn prism_form_field_number_value(f: PrismFormField, default_val: int) -> int
    match f.kind
        FormFieldNumber(v, _, _) => v
        _ => default_val

fn prism_form_field_number_min(f: PrismFormField, default_val: int) -> int
    match f.kind
        FormFieldNumber(_, lo, _) => lo
        _ => default_val

fn prism_form_field_number_max(f: PrismFormField, default_val: int) -> int
    match f.kind
        FormFieldNumber(_, _, hi) => hi
        _ => default_val

fn prism_form_field_range_value(f: PrismFormField, default_val: int) -> int
    match f.kind
        FormFieldRange(v, _, _, _) => v
        _ => default_val

fn prism_form_field_range_min(f: PrismFormField, default_val: int) -> int
    match f.kind
        FormFieldRange(_, lo, _, _) => lo
        _ => default_val

fn prism_form_field_range_max(f: PrismFormField, default_val: int) -> int
    match f.kind
        FormFieldRange(_, _, hi, _) => hi
        _ => default_val

fn prism_form_field_range_step(f: PrismFormField, default_val: int) -> int
    match f.kind
        FormFieldRange(_, _, _, s) => s
        _ => default_val

fn prism_form_field_bool_value(f: PrismFormField, default_val: bool) -> bool
    match f.kind
        FormFieldToggle(state) => state
        _ => default_val

fn prism_form_field_choice_options(f: PrismFormField) -> list
    match f.kind
        FormFieldChoice(options, _) => options
        _ => []

fn prism_form_field_choice_selected(f: PrismFormField, default_val: string) -> string
    match f.kind
        FormFieldChoice(_, sel) => sel
        _ => default_val

// ── Form ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// `fields` stays bare `list` (matching prism_pick/prism_tabs/prism_each's own convention for a
// "list of a same-file struct") with a runtime type_name guard, since PrismFormField is a STRUCT
// (type_name is verified reliable for structs throughout prism/widget/, unlike the enum case
// `rules` above is deliberately typed to avoid).
fn prism_form(fields: list) -> Result<PrismForm>
    let seen = {}
    let mut idx = 0
    for f in fields
        if type_name(f) != "PrismFormField"
            return err("prism_form: field " + str(idx) + " is not a PrismFormField (got " + type_name(f) + ")")
        if f.name in seen
            return err("prism_form: duplicate field name '" + f.name + "'")
        seen[f.name] = true
        idx += 1
    ok(PrismForm(fields))

fn prism_form_fields(form: PrismForm) -> list
    form.fields

fn prism_form_field_count(form: PrismForm) -> int
    len(form.fields)

// ── Per-kind rule checkers (private) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Each builds and returns its OWN local list of violations for one field (never mutates a list
// across a function boundary) -- the caller (prism_form_validate) folds these into its own
// accumulator with a plain for-loop + push, avoiding any assumption about list-argument aliasing.

fn _prism_form_check_stringy(field: PrismFormField, value: string) -> list
    let errs = []
    for r in field.rules
        match r
            FormRuleRequired() =>
                if value == ""
                    errs.push(PrismFormError(field.name, field.label + " is required"))
            FormRuleMinLength(n) =>
                if len(value) < n
                    errs.push(PrismFormError(field.name, field.label + " must be at least " + str(n) + " characters"))
            FormRuleMaxLength(n) =>
                if len(value) > n
                    errs.push(PrismFormError(field.name, field.label + " must be at most " + str(n) + " characters"))
    errs

fn _prism_form_check_choice(field: PrismFormField, selected: string) -> list
    let errs = []
    for r in field.rules
        match r
            FormRuleRequired() =>
                if selected == ""
                    errs.push(PrismFormError(field.name, field.label + " requires a selection"))
            FormRuleMinLength(_) => 0
            FormRuleMaxLength(_) => 0
    errs

// Required on a toggle means "must be true" -- the common "must accept terms" pattern.
fn _prism_form_check_toggle(field: PrismFormField, state: bool) -> list
    let errs = []
    for r in field.rules
        match r
            FormRuleRequired() =>
                if not state
                    errs.push(PrismFormError(field.name, field.label + " must be accepted"))
            FormRuleMinLength(_) => 0
            FormRuleMaxLength(_) => 0
    errs

// ── Validation ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Number's inherent [min_val, max_val] is checked here (not at construction -- see header); Range
// is never checked here because prism_form_range already guarantees its value is in-bounds at
// construction time, and (per the compatibility table) Range accepts none of the 3 rule kinds.
fn prism_form_validate(form: PrismForm) -> Result<list>
    let errors = []
    for field in form.fields
        match field.kind
            FormFieldText(v) =>
                for e in _prism_form_check_stringy(field, v)
                    errors.push(e)
            FormFieldEmail(v) =>
                for e in _prism_form_check_stringy(field, v)
                    errors.push(e)
            FormFieldPassword(v) =>
                for e in _prism_form_check_stringy(field, v)
                    errors.push(e)
            FormFieldChoice(_, sel) =>
                for e in _prism_form_check_choice(field, sel)
                    errors.push(e)
            FormFieldToggle(state) =>
                for e in _prism_form_check_toggle(field, state)
                    errors.push(e)
            FormFieldNumber(v, lo, hi) =>
                if v < lo or v > hi
                    errors.push(PrismFormError(field.name, field.label + " must be between " + str(lo) + " and " + str(hi)))
            FormFieldRange(_, _, _, _) => 0
    ok(errors)

fn prism_form_error_field(e: PrismFormError) -> string
    e.field_name

fn prism_form_error_message(e: PrismFormError) -> string
    e.message

// ── Rendering ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Composes ONLY from widget/prism_interact (entry/pick/flag/range) + widget/prism_arrange (stack)
// + widget/prism_content (label) -- no new PrismNodeKind, no markup, per axiom A7 / §15.1.

// Just the input widget for one field -- no label/help/error wrapping. Exposed publicly (not
// `_prism_form_*`) because it is a genuinely useful, independently composable building block for
// a caller assembling a custom layout, not merely an internal step.
fn prism_form_render_input(field: PrismFormField) -> Result<PrismNode>
    match field.kind
        FormFieldText(v) => prism_interact.prism_entry(field.label, prism_interact.prism_entry_kind_text(), v)
        FormFieldEmail(v) => prism_interact.prism_entry(field.label, prism_interact.prism_entry_kind_email(), v)
        FormFieldPassword(v) => prism_interact.prism_entry(field.label, prism_interact.prism_entry_kind_password(), v)
        FormFieldNumber(v, _, _) => prism_interact.prism_entry(field.label, prism_interact.prism_entry_kind_number(), str(v))
        FormFieldRange(v, lo, hi, step) => prism_interact.prism_range(field.label, lo, hi, step, v)
        FormFieldChoice(options, selected) => prism_interact.prism_pick(options, selected)
        FormFieldToggle(state) => prism_interact.prism_flag(field.label, state)

// One row: label + input + help (if any) + error (if any), in that order. error_msg == "" means
// no error to show for this field.
fn _prism_form_render_row(field: PrismFormField, error_msg: string) -> Result<PrismNode>
    let label_r = prism_content.prism_label(field.label)
    if is_err(label_r)
        return label_r
    let input_r = prism_form_render_input(field)
    if is_err(input_r)
        return input_r
    let parts = [unwrap(label_r), unwrap(input_r)]
    if field.help != ""
        let help_r = prism_content.prism_label(field.help)
        if is_err(help_r)
            return help_r
        parts.push(unwrap(help_r))
    if error_msg != ""
        let error_r = prism_content.prism_label(error_msg)
        if is_err(error_r)
            return error_r
        parts.push(unwrap(error_r))
    prism_arrange.prism_stack(parts)

// Validates first, then renders every field's row with its own first violated-rule message (if
// any) attached. A field can have multiple violations (prism_form_validate reports all of them);
// only the first, in rule-declaration order, is shown per row -- ordinary form UX, and NOVA dicts
// preserve insertion order (§15.9) so "first seen" is deterministic.
fn prism_form_render(form: PrismForm) -> Result<PrismNode>
    let errs_r = prism_form_validate(form)
    if is_err(errs_r)
        return err(unwrap_err(errs_r))
    let errs = unwrap(errs_r)
    let first_error = {}
    for e in errs
        if e.field_name not in first_error
            first_error[e.field_name] = e.message
    let rows = []
    for field in form.fields
        let msg = ""
        if field.name in first_error
            msg = first_error[field.name]
        let row_r = _prism_form_render_row(field, msg)
        if is_err(row_r)
            return row_r
        rows.push(unwrap(row_r))
    prism_arrange.prism_stack(rows)
